                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#sets_groupoids_or_categories"} Sets, groupoids, or categories?
              %li
                %a{:href => "#morphisms_of_ionads"} Morphisms of ionads
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_topos_of_opens"} The topos of opens
              %li
                %a{:href => "#bases_of_ionad_structures"} Bases of ionad structures
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            As a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorified
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
            developed the theory of ionads, in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^X
            plays an role analogous to that of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}^X
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorifying
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝟚
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}\in Set
            to the “categorified subobject classifier”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set\in Cat
            (which classifies
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+opfibrations"} discrete opfibrations
          %p
            The word ‘ionad’ is Irish for a location, place, or site; ‘Ionad’ often translates ‘Centre’ in titles of institutions. It is pronounced /ˈɪnəd/ (roughly ‘INN-ad’ or ‘UNN-ad’, not ‘i-NAD’ or ‘yonad’; ‘ЫН-ад’ in a North Slavic language), or more precisely ˈɨ̞n̪ˠəd̪ˠeast in Munster), following
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://secure.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/wiki/Irish_phonology"} Wikipedia
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
            on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            can be defined by giving its interior operator, an operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int\colon \mathbb{2}^X \to \mathbb{2}^X
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝟚
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}
            is the poset of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊇
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \supseteq Int(A)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊇
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(Int(A)) \supseteq Int(A)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(X) = X
              , and
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(A \cap B) = Int(A) \cap Int(B)
          %p
            for all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In more sophisticated language,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int
            is a finite
            = succeed "-preserving" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet"} meet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} Categorifying
            (mostly), we have this:
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong ionad
              is a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              together with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} finite limit-preserving
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Int
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int_X
              on the category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^X
              %strong bounded
              if the comonad is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+monad"} accessible
          %p
            Although Garner does not require an ionad to be bounded, the nicest results hold for them, and all of his applications involve only bounded ionads. In fact, Garner writes, ‘Indeed, the existence of unbounded ionads is a problem that seems to be independent of the axioms of
            = succeed ".\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zermelo-Fraenkel+set+theory"} Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory
          .un_defn
            %h6#remark_garner_remark_39 Remark (Garner Remark 3.9)
            %p
              Bounded ionads give rise to exactly the Grothendieck toposes with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos"} enough points
              as their topos of coalgebras. (See below)
          %h3#sets_groupoids_or_categories Sets, groupoids, or categories?
          %p
            It may seem odd to take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to be a
            %em set
            rather than something else such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a groupoid or a category, of course, but the reason for taking it to be a set is that it makes the analogy to classical topological spaces closer. Consider the following three notions:
          %ol
            %li
              A set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              together with a finite-limit-preserving comonad on its powerset
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝟚
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}^X
            %li
              A set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              equipped with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              (which we can regard as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
              that happens to be symmetric) together with a finite-limit-preserving comonad on the hom-preorder
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝟚
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}^X
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              together with a finite-limit-preserving comonad on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝟚
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}^X
          %p
            All three of these
            %em induce
            a topology on the underlying set of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em exactly
            a topological space: (2) and (3) include the extra data of a (perhaps symmetric) preorder on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that maps bijectively-on-objects into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/specialization+preorder"} specialization preorder
            of that topology.
          %p
            However, as in other cases such as
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"} Segal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+spaces"} complete Segal spaces
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
          %ul
            %li
              A structure as in (2) above, but such that the given equivalence relation coincides with the relation “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              are in all the same open sets” (which it automatically
              = succeed ")." do
                %em implies
            %li A structure as in (3) above, but such that the given preorder coincides with the specialization preorder.
          %p
            These would give equivalent definitions to (1), but may be better-behaved in some ways. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            without
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets+cover"} sets cover
          %ul
            %li
              A groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with a finite-limit-preserving comonad on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^X
              such that the induced functor from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to the category of points of the resulting topos is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonic+functor"} pseudomonic
            %li
              A category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with a finite-limit-preserving comonad on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^X
              such that the induced functor from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to the category of points of the resulting topos is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful"} fully faithful
          %p
            (Asking that these functors also be surjective on objects would be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+space"} sobriety
            condition on an ionad.)
          %p
            When categorifying further to “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-ionads” and “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-ionads”, the possible options multiply further; but that is probably a topic for another page. For discussion of these questions, see the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/5857"} nForum thread
          %h2#morphisms_of_ionads Morphisms of ionads
          %p
            Recall that, given two topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            (on the
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*(Int(A)) \subseteq Int(f^*(A))
          %p
            for every subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            takes the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
          %p Categorifying (and adding a coherence law), we have this:
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Given ionads
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %strong continuous map
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              consists of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
              (on the underlying sets of points) together with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Int
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi Int
                      %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Int
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int_f\colon f^* \circ Int_Y \to Int_X \circ f^* ,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*\colon Set^Y \to Set^X
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Int
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int_f
              ‘respects the comonad structures’.
          .query
            %p I need to figure out exactly what this last clause means.
          %p
            Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Int
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int_f
            is part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} structure
            here; it is not merely a property. (In other words, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            from ionads to topological spaces is not
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
          %p
            There is an obvious notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            were allowed to be a groupoid). However, the category of ionads is presumably (like
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+prosetal+2-category"} locally prosetal 2-category
            under the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/specialisation+order"} specialisation order
          %h2#the_topos_of_opens The topos of opens
          %p
            As every topological space has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            (in fact a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            (in fact a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Given an ionad
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %strong open
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is simply a
              %span.newWikiWord
                coalgebra
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/algebra+of+a+monad"} ?
              of the comonad
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Int
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int_X
          %p
            The opens of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            form a topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective+geometric+morphism"} surjective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            together with a surjective geometric morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^X
            to some topos, much as a topological space may be defined as a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            together with a surjective locale morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}^X
            to some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)
            is essentially unique by surjectivity, and this also shows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)
            has
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enough+points"} enough points
          %p
            Just as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a topological space, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)
            should be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is an ionad. In fact, this holds only for
            %em bounded
            ionads; an ionad may be defined to be bounded if and only if its topos of opens is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            (or equivalently a Grothendieck topos).
          %p
            A continous map between topological spaces may be given by a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            and a commuting square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝟚
                            %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi f
                                %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝟚
                            %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \\Omega(Y)    &amp; \to              &amp; \Omega(X) \\
                  \\downarrow   &amp;                  &amp; \downarrow \\
                  \\mathbb{2}^Y &amp; \overset{f^*}\to &amp; \mathbb{2}^X
                  }
          %p
            Similarly, a continuous map between ionads may be given by a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            and a commuting square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Set
                            %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi f
                                %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Set
                            %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \\Omega(Y)    &amp; \to              &amp; \Omega(X) \\
                  \\downarrow   &amp;                  &amp; \downarrow \\
                  Set^Y        &amp; \overset{f^*}\to &amp; Set^X
                  }
          %p
            Without loss of generality, we may require this square to commute on the nose; this is related to the triviality of
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            Note that the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(Y) \to \Omega(X)
            must be the preimage half of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X)
            to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            to be such a geometric morphism together with a compatible map between the generating points of the toposes.
          %h2#bases_of_ionad_structures Bases of ionad structures
          %p …
          %h2#references References
          %p Ionads have been introduced and studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"}> Richard Garner
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Ionads
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0912.1415"} arXiv
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2012.02.013"} doi
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://comp.mq.edu.au/~rgarner/Papers/Ionads.pdf"} author-archived version of published copy
          %p
            Some basic aspects of the theory are developed there, and applications to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            are discussed.
          %p Further aspects of the theory of ionads, with a focus on their logical importance were studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Di+Liberti"}> Ivan Di Liberti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Towards Higher Topology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Di+Liberti"}> Ivan Di Liberti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formal Model Theory &amp; Higher Topology
        .revisedby
